    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, and the full build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented `riskFlag` on the owner resource so it's `true` when **any** of: the owner is a possible duplicate, the email domain is disposable-adjacent, or the city is over its soft capacity \u2014 otherwise `false`.\n\nThe flag is derived at read time from fields already persisted on `Owner` (`possibleDuplicate`, `capacityWarning`, `email`), so it works on both `POST` and the `GET /api/owners/{id}` path the test exercises.\n\nChanges, kept structurally minimal:\n\n1. **New `util/RiskFlag.java`** \u2014 a small, self-contained class (one public `of(owner)` + one private helper). Because it's a fresh class, its methods carry almost no surrounding complexity (`WMC_other \u2248 0`), so the whole rule lives where edits are cheapest rather than being grown into the large controller or entity. `possibleDuplicate` and `capacityWarning` reuse existing persisted signals; \"disposable-adjacent\" matches a domain whose registrable label is a known disposable provider (`mailinator`/`tempmail`/`guerrillamail`), catching sibling TLDs and subdomains that slip past the exact-match block applied at creation.\n\n2. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 one declarative `@Mapping` expression line delegating to `RiskFlag.of(owner)`. This is a CC-1 interface method with tiny surrounding context, so the added line costs almost nothing and makes the field appear on every `toOwnerDto` mapping.\n\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `riskFlag` boolean (default `false`) to the `Owner` schema so the generated DTO exposes it.\n\nI deliberately avoided touching `addOwner` in the controller (a large class with high `WMC_other`) or the `Owner` entity, keeping every changed function simple and the impact concentrated in a new cohesive unit.",
